    (
      number: "14",
      title: "DO UNITED STATES RESIDENTS HAVE SPECIFIC PRIVACY RIGHTS?",
      body:
          "Your rights depend on the law that applies to you and to our processing. Where applicable, these may include access, correction, deletion, portability, non-discrimination, and opting out of sale, sharing, targeted advertising, or certain profiling. A law's inclusion in this notice does not establish that every threshold or provision applies to 1touch.\n\nCollection and disclosure inventory\n\nDevelopment testing has involved personal information. The following inventory describes confirmed account and community records. Advertising and notification information is additionally described in section 19. The inventory is not a claim that every example within a statutory category is collected.\n\nCategory: Identifiers and contact/account records (A/B)\nConfirmed examples: Email addresses, usernames, nicknames, account and social-provider identifiers\nRecipients and retention: AWS hosting and account email delivery; relevant sign-in providers when used. Active account records until account deletion; backup and technical records follow their separate schedules.\n\nCategory: Internet or network activity (F)\nConfirmed examples: Community interactions, follows, prediction/points activity, notification and request records\nRecipients and retention: AWS hosting and, for push delivery, Firebase Cloud Messaging. Retention depends on the record, as described below.\n\nCategory: Visual/electronic information (H)\nConfirmed examples: Uploaded profile and community images\nRecipients and retention: Cloudflare R2; published media can also be seen by users. Unattached media: seven-day schedule; published media: until removed; profile photo: until replaced or account deletion, followed by queued file deletion.\n\nCategory: Authentication credentials (L, where the statutory definition applies)\nConfirmed examples: Account login credentials; stored password and session-token hashes\nRecipients and retention: AWS hosting processes stored authentication records. Passwords are stored as hashes. Account records are removed on deletion; sessions have a thirty-day validity period and are separately cleaned up.\n\nSources are your entries and activity, device settings and requests, and the optional providers described in this notice. We also process videos and optional calendar information when those features are used. We do not sell account information or community content to data brokers for money. Advertising data disclosures are described separately and are not treated as absent merely because ads are non-personalized. Contact us to exercise any sale, sharing, or targeted-advertising opt-out right that applies to you.\n\nRequests and appeals\n\nContact support@1touch.football to exercise applicable rights or appeal a decision. We may verify identity or an authorized agent's authority using information proportionate to the request. Do not send passwords or authentication codes. We will explain any refusal and applicable appeal route and respond within the period required by the law that applies."
