    (
      number: "19",
      title: "NON-PERSONALIZED ADVERTISING AND NOTIFICATIONS",
      body:
          "1touch's advertising model uses non-personalized advertisements through Google AdMob. Non-personalized advertising does not mean that no data is processed: Google's Mobile Ads SDK may process IP addresses, approximate location inferred from IP addresses, device identifiers, advertising interactions, and diagnostic information to deliver ads, measure performance, and prevent fraud. Non-personalized ads use contextual information rather than your past behavior for ad selection. Identifiers can still be used for frequency limits and aggregate reporting. Required advertising consent and device permissions apply separately. 1touch does not request access to your device's precise GPS location. For push notifications, we process a generated installation identifier, notification token, device platform, language setting, and notification preferences using Firebase Cloud Messaging. You can manage notification permissions in your device settings.\n\nWhere provided, open Settings → About → Ad privacy choices to revisit the Google consent form. Your device settings also control notification permissions and supported advertising identifier controls. If a choice is unavailable or you need help, contact us. Refusing an advertising consent is separate from account deletion. Provider explanations: AdMob Android data (https://developers.google.com/admob/android/privacy/play-data-disclosure), AdMob iOS data (https://developers.google.com/admob/ios/privacy/data-disclosure), and Google partner-service information (https://policies.google.com/technologies/partner-sites)."
    ),
    (
      number: "20",
      title: "HOSTING AND OPERATIONAL RETENTION",
      body:
          "Our application server and account database are hosted using Amazon Web Services Lightsail in Seoul, South Korea. Account verification and recovery emails use Amazon Simple Email Service in the Seoul region. Profile photographs and community media are stored using Cloudflare R2. Cloudflare identifies our R2 bucket location as Asia-Pacific (APAC); this regional designation does not identify a specific storage country. Daily database backup files are subject to a fourteen-day retention schedule. Manual and deployment database backups are subject to a thirty-day retention schedule. Expired backups are removed during scheduled backup processing after a new backup has been successfully created and verified. Information associated with a deleted account may remain in backup copies until they are removed under the applicable retention schedule. Container service logs are rotated according to storage size rather than a fixed number of days. Account deletion and the treatment of published community content are described in the account deletion section of this policy. Contact support@1touch.football for a request concerning your personal information in retained copies.\n\nRecord-specific retention and deletion\n\nRecord: Verification codes and sessions\nCurrent behavior: Verification codes expire after ten minutes and are removed when consumed. Sessions expire after thirty days and are removed on logout. The hourly maintenance job removes expired codes and sessions; expiration and physical deletion are separate events.\n\nRecord: Abuse-prevention rate limits\nCurrent behavior: Hashed scopes whose window began more than one hour ago are eligible for the hourly cleanup.\n\nRecord: Unpublished drafts and unattached files\nCurrent behavior: Seven days from the last draft save or file upload, followed by scheduled cleanup. Failed object deletions remain queued for another attempt.\n\nRecord: Published posts and comments\nCurrent behavior: Deleting a post clears its title/body and queues attached files for deletion; deleting a comment clears its body. Structural records can remain for thread relationships. Deleting an account instead removes its author association and may leave published content in place.\n\nRecord: Points, predictions, follows, blocks and notification preferences\nCurrent behavior: Account-associated records are removed on account deletion. An expired notification's delivery window does not mean its database record has been deleted.\n\nRecord: Push registration\nCurrent behavior: Removed on unregistering, logout/session deletion, or account deletion. Revoking OS notification permission alone is not a promise that all server notification records are erased immediately.\n\nRecord: Moderation reports\nCurrent behavior: Resolved reports become eligible for deletion by the hourly maintenance job 180 days after resolution (approximately six months). The job processes records in limited batches, so eligibility and physical deletion are separate events. Unresolved reports remain pending review. Account deletion clears reporter/reviewer account associations but may leave the report itself. Deleting a report does not delete the underlying post, comment, or chat message.\n\nRecord: Social-provider deletion callbacks\nCurrent behavior: Provider and hashed event identifier are retained to detect duplicate callbacks. The receipt does not store the original callback token or deleted account identifier. No automatic expiry is implemented.\n\nSupport correspondence. Our retention policy is to delete ordinary support correspondence 180 days after the inquiry is resolved. An unresolved inquiry remains open. We may retain only the records necessary for a specific legal duty, dispute, or privacy-rights request for the applicable period. This is our mailbox handling rule, not a claim that Porkbun erases every backup at the same time.\n\nProvider copies. Removing a push registration from our database does not automatically erase Google's Firebase installation ID. Google's published FCM rule retains that ID until an installation-deletion API request; after such a request, removal from live and backup systems can take up to 180 days. Account deletion in 1touch does not currently initiate that separate installation-deletion request. Contact us for help with provider-held information. Cloudflare's data processing terms address deletion or return when the relevant service ends, with legal exceptions; they do not give us a verified fixed time for physical erasure of every copy after an individual file is deleted."
